/* book Worked example 2: Q: At what overdrive Vov would the nMOS and the NPN have equal transconductance at equal current?
   1. Set IC / VT = 2 IC / Vov. The current cancels.
   2. So Vov = 2 VT = 2 × 26 mV = 52 mV.
   3. The square law would hand the MOSFET the larger gm below 52 mV, but there the MOSFET is close to weak inversion, where the square law fails and gm = IC/(n·VT) with n about 1.3 to 1.5, still below the bipolar. Real overdrives are 200–500 mV, so the bipolar gives 4 to 10 times the transconductance.
   Answer: The square law gives equal gm at Vov = 52 mV, and the ratio Vov / (2 VT) does not depend on current; in practice the bipolar has the larger gm at any equal current. */
