/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A layout is a stack of rectangles, one set per layer.
   - A rule is a margin bought against these errors.
   - A layout that breaks a rule is rejected before fabrication.
   - Writing every rule in micrometres makes the rule set useless the moment the process shrinks.
   - L is the drawn length in micrometres, n is the rule expressed in λ (a pure number), and λ is the unit length in micrometres, often half the smallest drawn feature.
   - A layout drawn in λ can be moved to a new process by changing one number.
   - λ is not the wavelength of the lithography light.
   - Every rule set, however large, is built from a few families.
   - Minimum pitch is not a separate rule; it follows from the other two.
   - A design rule checker (DRC) reads the layout file, rebuilds each layer from the drawn shapes, and tests every rule on every pair of shapes.
*/
